From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:13:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Optionally ring render doorbells once
 per group

Every render publication posts a TA and an FS submit record and rings
both pipes, and each doorbell starts a firmware scheduler call, which
reads the ring cursors once and launches the renders visible then. A
per-record model of firmware traces at render depth 64 puts those
scheduler calls at about 8.4 us per render on 1.1075 with 8 render
units against 3.6 on macOS 26B123; a second model of the same traces
gives 9.6 against 6.9. With 16 render units the first model gives 4.1,
at an estimated 887 MiB per deep VM, so ringing less often is the way
that costs no memory.

Add g16_render_bell_group (runtime, default 0). With k from 2 to 16, a
render publication made while at least k renders are already in flight
posts its records without ringing, and the k-th such publication rings
both pipes for all of them, so one scheduler call can take the group.
A publication made with fewer than k in flight rings as before, which
also covers owed records, and every publish pass, which the poll pass
runs after each retirement, rings owed records once fewer than k renders
are in flight, so a record is never left unrung as the queue drains.
At a fixed k at most k - 1 records are owed. A smaller k written while
records are owed can leave k or more of them owed, all in flight and
none started, so the publish pass also rings once k or more are owed.
With g16_onebell set, or before both render queues are registered,
nothing changes. The pure rules are in bell_group.rs; the statistics
line "render_bell: group K owed O rung R deferred D paid P settled S"
counts each case.

Assisted-by: LLM
